<p>Different sports have different economic models, and different demographics.</p>
<p>One of the interesting things about rugby, especially in comparison to, say, football, is that the focus - and the money - is all on the international game.  With a couple of exceptions (France and Japan), the domestic game is not profitable and has to be subsidized by owners or the international game.  There is not the fierce tribal loyalty that marks out fans of football clubs.  And the crowd numbers follow suit.  There are no 80,000 seat stadia for domestic rugby clubs.</p>
<p>But the international game attracts those audiences, both in person and on TV.  And yet the people in charge of the game still fail to recognize that if they want to grow the sport - both in terms of revenue and interest - it is those games that need to be played.  There is not the same "club/country" row that there is in football, but there is a question about how the schedule should be organized to best benefit the needs of the domestic leagues, especially across the northern and southern hemispheres, where obviously the seasons are out of sync.</p>
<p>A radical suggestion: don't.</p>
<p>Instead, acknowledge that the interest and money is all in the international game, and do more of that. Specifically, play internationals year-round.  Have international squads be separate from the domestic game.  The domestic game offers a "player pathway" to the international game, and players who need "time off" or who are rehabbing from injury should be able to join a club in their domestic league for a portion of the season, but otherwise the two are basically separate concerns.  There should not be any question of whether a player is available to play for a club or if he is "on international duty".  It's one or the other.</p>
<p>How would this work?  Well, I would divide the year into four six week "series" - one in the classic Six Nations window (February/March), one in the "southern tour" window (May/June), one in the Rugby Championship window (August/September) and one in the "northern tour" window (November).  The exact weeks would depend on everything else that was going on, but these should be clearly demarcated with at least a month between "seasons" or "tournaments".</p>
<p>Keeping on with not worrying too much about the actual <i>details</i>, I'd like to provide an outline of how I'd see this working.</p>
<p>The first question is: <i>who</i>?  Which teams would take part.  There are 10 obvious candidates: the six from the Six Nations and the four from the Rugby Championship.  At this point it makes sense to add Japan into the mix; adding one more takes the total to twelve, which is a nice round number.  While there are many worthy candidates, I'd be tempted to add a "rest of the world" team showcasing the best players from all the countries <i>not</i> represented.  I'd hope such a team - playing internationals year round - would be able to gel and compete in spite of issues of language and culture.</p>
<p>The obvious format is a round-robin with each pair of teams playing each other twice.  But, to channel the money demon, I don't think that's the best idea.  Some of these match-ups are genuine box office, while others would be sloppy and one-sided.  I'd rather avoid that.  Personally, I would pay to watch New Zealand and South Africa go at it week-in, week-out; but in reality, I think they should probably play each other once in each series.  The same is probably true for other groups and match-ups: whether they are grudge matches such as "Le Crunch" between France and England, or just match-ups based on parity (say Argentina v Japan).  Each pair of teams should play each other once during the course of the year (11 matches for each team), but the other 13 matches should be chosen to maximise excitement and viewer numbers, but with the caveat that no tie should be played more than once per series.</p>
<p>The next question is :<i>where</i> should these games be played?  Again, following the money, wherever they will garner the most revenue.  It makes sense to me that countries would want to play their big matches at home, and they should play at least some of them there every year.  But there should also be room in the calendar for a lot of these matches - and, in particular, the ones that have name recognition - to be played around the world.  New Zealand-South Africa, England-France and some Ireland games should be played in countries like the US where "the spectacle overshadows the match".  Wherever the games are played, I would expect them to be carried on TV (or streaming) around the world.  While most of the games in any given series would be played in "home countries", I would like to see at least one game every week played elsewhere and for every country to play at least one game in a neutral venue every series.</p>
<p>A lot of the focus for this is money: the game needs money brought in so that players and coaches (and others) can be played and so that the game can be developed and grow.  With that in mind, the money raised from both TV rights and tickets should be distributed to <b>all</b> the competing nations and to domestic leagues around the world.  Exactly how that would be worked out is a detail too far and I'm sure would be very contentious.  But at all times this must be a distribution for the benefit of all, not just the stars or the rich countries.  There has been a lot of chatter over the years about the All Blacks charging an "appearance fee", but it is one of the few ways in which they can access the kind of money that is on offer in other countries with larger populations.  This would go away in the face of a fairer distribution of income.  As with every other aspect of life, whatever formula is decided should be made public and, along with a continually update set of accounts, should be made available on World Rugby's website.</p>
<p>I have written elsewhere about player welfare and sustainability, but assuming 24 internationals in a year, each country could select 38 or so players to form a squad for each six-week series.  Ideally, I would not like to see them call up any "injury replacements" or otherwise change the squad in that time, but (details, details) there would obviously need to be some such facility for "extreme" cases.  If selected for all four series, these players would not play in any other games in the year.  But in no case could a player be selected for a match if they have already played 24 matches (at any level) in the past year.</p>
<p>Finally, having written extensively about what would happen at the international level, I want to make the point that it would be up to each nation to decide on its own domestic schedule, competitions and rules.  With the caveat that four times a year some (small number of) players would be selected to go and join the national squad, the international game would not impact on the domestic game at all.</p>
